## 3.7 Best Practices

### Always Use `!void` for Main (Unless Trivial)

Even if you think your program cannot fail, start with `!void`. It costs nothing and makes it easy to add fallible operations later.

```zig
pub fn main() !void {
    // Your code
}
```

### Use `GeneralPurposeAllocator` for Development

```zig
var gpa = std.heap.GeneralPurposeAllocator(.{}){};
defer _ = gpa.deinit();
const allocator = gpa.allocator();
```

At the end of the program, `gpa.deinit()` will return `true` if any memory was leaked. This catches leaks before they reach production. In release builds, you can switch to `std.heap.page_allocator` for minimal overhead.

### Prefer `std.debug.print` Only for Temporary Debugging

`std.debug.print` is convenient, but it prints to stderr and can truncate output. For proper output, use `stdout.writer()`:

```zig
const stdout = std.io.getStdOut().writer();
try stdout.print("Value: {d}\n", .{x});
```

### Free Resources in Reverse Order

When you have multiple `defer` statements, they execute in reverse of their declaration order. Use this to avoid use‑after‑free:

```zig
const buffer = try allocator.alloc(u8, 1024);
defer allocator.free(buffer);

const args = try std.process.argsAlloc(allocator);
defer std.process.argsFree(allocator, args); // runs before buffer's free
```

### Use `errdefer` for Partial Cleanup

If you allocate resources that may need cleanup only on error paths, combine `errdefer` with a flag:

```zig
var resource_acquired = false;
errdefer if (resource_acquired) cleanup();
try acquireResource();
resource_acquired = true;
```

### Compile with Safety Checks During Development

Always compile with `-O Debug` (the default) or `-O ReleaseSafe` when testing. Debug mode enables bounds checking, integer overflow detection, and other safety features that catch bugs early.

